"Ms. Asiya Jamil vs The Canara Bank on 8 April, 2009 (https://indiankanoon.org/doc/65127929/)\nciteturn68search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; The respondent bank has contested the present petition ­ 3 ­ and filed the written statement in which it has been submitted that in exercise of powers conferred under sub section (4) of Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act 1949, Reserve Bank of India prepared a scheme for amalgamation of the Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with Canara Bank and the said scheme sanctioned by the Central Government under Section 45 (7) of the said Act and was notified in the gazette of India dated 23.08.1985 and the said amalgamation is not a transfer of tenancy and does not amount to an act of sub­letting, assignment or parting with the premises and therefore present petition is not maintainable on the ground of sub­letting u/s 14 (1) (b) of DRC Act and is liable to be dismissed. ... It has been submitted that the respondent bank has been in exclusive possession of tenanted premises since last 22 years in its own capacity and therefore there has been a implied consent of the landlord/petitioner for creation of tenancy in favour of the respondent.\n3. The respondent bank has contested the present petition ­ 3 ­ and filed the written statement in which it has been submitted that in exercise of powers conferred under sub section (4) of Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act 1949, Reserve Bank of India prepared a scheme for amalgamation of the Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with Canara Bank and the said scheme sanctioned by the Central Government under Section 45 (7) of the said Act and was notified in the gazette of India dated 23.08.1985 and the said amalgamation is not a transfer of tenancy and does not amount to an act of sub­letting, assignment or parting with the premises and therefore present petition is not maintainable on the ground of sub­letting u/s 14 (1) (b) of DRC Act and is liable to be dismissed. It has been contended that petitioner has thorough knowledge that the premises in suit has been continuously in occupation of the respondent since the amalgamation of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with Canara Bank as the petitioner has been continuously residing in the same locality/area where the premises in suit is situated and the petitioner and her representative have been regularly visiting the premises in suit and have never raised any objection to the premises being occupied by the respondent as a tenant. It has been submitted that the respondent bank has been in exclusive possession of tenanted premises since last 22 years in its own capacity and therefore there has been a implied consent of the landlord/petitioner for creation of tenancy in favour of the respondent. It is further contended that the petitioner was maintaining a saving account along with Mst. Atiya Rehman in their joint names with the respondent bank at Chandni Chowk Branch and the rent @ Rs. 240/­ per month has been credited in to the said Saving Account of the petitioner and the said rent was withdrawn from time to time by the petitioner and as such by her ­ 4 ­ conduct , the petitioner had impliedly accepted the respondent as her tenant and it cannot be alleged at this stage after the gap of 22 years by the landlord/ petitioner that the respondent bank is a sub­tenant in the tenanted premises. It has been further contended that there is no cause of action on the ground u/s 14 (1) (f) of DRC Act and it has been submitted that the tenanted premises are being used as a godown by the respondent bank which is opened regularly by the bank officials and the same do not require any substantial repairs and are not in a dangerous condition and moreover no such document has been filed by the petitioner to show that she had applied to Local Authority for getting the Plans sanctioned or that the Local Authority had issued any notice to the petitioner to carry out repairs on account of unsafe condition of the premises or that she has necessary funds for reconstruction. It has been further contended that present eviction petition filed by the petitioner is time barred as the petitioner/landlord did not take any action for years against the respondent bank and therefore the tenancy rights accrued in favour of the respondent. It has been further contended that the petitioner only wants to evict the respondent as she wants to let out the tenanted premise at a higher price and the respondent being the old tenant is an hurdle for the same. The respondent has prayed for dismissal of the petition.\n\n10. On the other hand the Ld. Counsel for the respondent has vehemently argued that under the scheme of amalgamation u/s (4) of Section 45 of Banking Regulation Act 1949, RBI prepared a scheme for amalgamation of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with the Canara Bank which was sanctioned by the Central Government and the same was notified in the Gazette of India dated 23.08.1985 and as such M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. merged with the Canara Bank under the legislative scheme and therefore it does not amount to subletting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises. Ld. Counsel for the respondent has vehemently contended that the respondent is not sub tenant under the petitioner as the M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. was merged with the Canara Bank in the year 1985 and the present petition has been filed after a gap of 22 years and moreover, the respondent bank has been paying the rent all along to the petitioner and the petitioner was also maintaining a saving ­ 7 ­ account with the Canara Bank since 1988. She has further argued that the petitioner knows that the respondent bank has taken over M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. and the respondent bank is also remitting the rentals in the account of the petitioner and petitioner has not raised any objections for the last 22 years and therefore by virtue of acquiescence, the respondent has become the tenant as the petitioner has by her conduct accepted the respondent as a tenant. The Ld. Counsel for the respondent has also argued that by virtue of aforesaid merger, all the assets and liabilities including the lease agreement, movable and immovable properties were taken over by the Canara Bank . In support of her contention, the Ld. Counsel for the respondent has relied upon in an authority titled as \"Mrs. Asha Rohtagi & Ors. Vs Erstwhile New Bank of India through General Manager PNB\". On the other hand, Ld. Counsel for the petitioner has argued that the authority relied upon by the Ld. Counsel for the respondent is not applicable in the facts and circumstances of the present case as the management of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. which was the tenant of the petitioner and the management of the Canara Bank are entirely different and in the authority relied upon by Ld. Counsel for the respondent, both the banks were Nationalised bank.\n\n11. Facts in the present case are within the narrow compass. The case of the petitioner is that M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. was the tenant and same has been merged with the Canara Bank without the consent of the petitioner and it amounts to subletting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises. On the other hand, the stand of the respondent bank is ­ 8 ­ that M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. has been merged with the Canara Bank under the legislative scheme by virtue of notification sanctioned by the Central Government u/s 45 (7) of Banking Regulation Act 1949 on 23.08.1985 and amalgamation under the legislative scheme does not amount to subletting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises.\n\n17. In view of aforesaid discussions and categorical law laid down by the Hon'b le High Court of Delhi in \"Mrs. Asha Rohtagi & Ors. Vs Erstwhile New Bank of India through General Manager PNB\", in the present case also the acquisition/merger of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with Canara Bank does not ­ 12 ­ amount to sub­letting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises as the merger/acquisition has been done under the legislative scheme. So far the contention of the Ld. Counsel for the petitioner that the management of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. and management of Canara Bank are different and therefore the law laid down by Hon'b le High Court of Delhi is not applicable in the present case, is without any merit. It makes hardly any difference that management of two banks is not the same as the scheme by virtue of which M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. has been merged into the Canara Bank is legislative in nature and therefore in view of law laid down by the Hon'b le High Court of Delhi it does not amount to sub­letting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises.\n\n18. I also find considerable force in the contention of the Ld. Counsel for the respondent that by way of her conduct the petitioner has impliedly accepted the respondent as a tenant. In the present case, admittedly the rentals of the tenanted premises is paid by the respondent bank to the petitioner. Admittedly, the petitioner has opened a saving account with the respondent bank in the year 1988 and the account opening form dated 04.07.08 is Ex. PW­1/R­1. Admittedly, the suit property is situated within the premises where the petitioner is residing. PW­1 has also admitted in her cross­examination that she has known about the merger of Laxmi Commercial Bank Ltd. for the last 15­16 years. The aforesaid statement of PW­1 shows that the petitioner is in the knowledge of existence of Canara Bank for the last 15­16 years. She is also maintaining a saving account with the respondent bank and she is also receiving rent from the respondent bank and she has never raised any objections to the occupation of the ­ 13 ­ respondent bank in the suit premises. This shows that the petitioner has acquiesced the respondent as a tenant.\n\n19. In view of aforesaid discussion, in my considered opinion, the petitioner has failed to prove that the merger/acquisition of M/s Lakshmi Commercial Bank Ltd. with the Canara Bank amounts to sub­letting, assigning or parting with the possession of the suit premises. Therefore, no ground is made out u/s 14 (1) (b) of DRC Act and present petition is dismissed under this ground.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMrs. Asha Rohtagi And Ors. vs Erstwhile New Bank Of India Through ... on 21 April, 2005 (https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/1033921/?formInput=amalgamation+++++++++doctypes%3A+sc%2Chighcourts)\nciteturn68search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Briefly narrated, facts leading to this petition are that on 3.10.96 the petitioner herein filed an eviction petition under section 14(1)(a)(b) of the Act against New Bank of India through General Manager and Punjab National Bank seeking their eviction from premises K-16, Chaudhary Building, Connaught Circus, New Delhi on the ground of non-payment of rent and sub-letting contemplated under clause (a) and (b) of proviso to sub section (1) of section 14 of the Act. ... Against this judgment and order, a revision was filed in the High Court of Andhra Pradesh, which was allowed and it was held that amalgamation of appellant 1 company with appellant 2 company on the basis of application made by the appellant 1 company by submitting a scheme which was duly approved and sanctioned by the High Court of Bombay was not an involuntary act and this order of amalgamation resulted in transfer of tenancy rights without any notice or opportunity to the landlord. ... The question which arose for consideration was whether involuntary transfer of lease rights from the foreign company to the Indian Company amounted to sub letting within the meaning of section 14(1)(b) of the Act.\n\n## Document Fragment View\n\n### Fragment Information\n\nShowing contexts for: amalgamation in Mrs. Asha Rohtagi And Ors. vs Erstwhile New Bank Of India Through ... on 21 April, 2005\n\n#### Context Size Options\n\nContext size:\n\n[Input] [Input] [Button: Small]\n\nCurrent\n\n[Input] [Input] [Button: Large]\n\n[Input] [Input] [Button: Larger]\n\n### Matching Fragments\n\n1. This petition under Article 227 of the Constitution of India is directed against order dated 15.2.2005 passed by Shri S.M. Chopra, Additional Rent Control Tribunal whereby tenant's appeal was allowed and the eviction order under section 14(1)(b) of Delhi Rent Control Act (for short the ' Act') passed by learned ARC vide order dated 24.11.2004 in eviction petition No. E-288/2004 was set aside.\n\n2. Briefly narrated, facts leading to this petition are that on 3.10.96 the petitioner herein filed an eviction petition under section 14(1)(a)(b) of the Act against New Bank of India through General Manager and Punjab National Bank seeking their eviction from premises K-16, Chaudhary Building, Connaught Circus, New Delhi on the ground of non-payment of rent and sub-letting contemplated under clause (a) and (b) of proviso to sub section (1) of section 14 of the Act. It was alleged in the eviction petition that the demised premises were let out to New Bank of India for commercial purposes and the respondent has failed to pay arrears of rent from October 1993 till date despite service of demand notice dated 10.10.94. It was further stated that respondent No. 1 has sublet, assigned or parted with the possession of the tenanted premises to the respondent No.2 through respondent No.3 without obtaining the written consent of the petitioner. The eviction petition was contested by respondent No. 2 and 3 who are Branch Manager and General Manager of the PNB. There was no appearance on behalf of respondent No.1. Respondent No. 2 and 3 in their written statement took the stand that respondent No.1 is no more a legal entity, the same having been amalgamated in Punjab National Bank under the Govt. of India notification dated 4.9.93 issued by Government of India under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980 under clause 4(2) whereof tenancy rights have been conferred on the Punjab National Bank. It was thus alleged that there has been no subletting, assignment or parting with the possession. Rather Punjab National Bank is a statutory successor of New Bank of India. The allegation regarding arrears of rent and service of demand notice was also denied. After considering the evidence led by the parties and the case laws cited at the Bar, learned ARC vide order dated 24.11.2004 held that the demand notice dated 10.10.94 was duly served and the respondents failed to clear the arrear of rent within two months from the service of notice and as such there was default in payment of rent. Learned ARC further came to the conclusion that there is no relationship of landlord and tenant between the petitioner and respondent/ PNB who are sub leasees or assignee and the original tenant NBI ceased to exist. Therefore, petition under section 14(1)(a) of the Act was dismissed. As regards grounds contemplated under section 14(1)(b) of the Act, learned ARC came to the conclusion that the merger/ amalgamation of NBI with PNB vide notification dated 4.9.93 issued under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980 amounts to subletting, assignment or parting with possession of the demised premises. In coming to this conclusion, learned ARC relied upon various authorities namely 113 (2004) DLT 80 (SC) titled as Singer India Ltd. v. Chander Mohan Chadha and Ors.; AIR 1980 SC 1655 titled as Paras Ram Harnand Raos. M/s Shanti Prasad Narinder Kumar Jain; 1988 (2) RLR page 449 titled as M/s Cox and Kings Agencies Ltd and Anr. v. Smt. Chander Malhotra; Vol X 1986 (2) All India Rent Control Journal page 413 titled as General Radio and Appliances Co. Ltd. and Ors. v. M.A. Khader by LRs. Feeling aggrieved by the eviction order, the respondent preferred appeal before learned Tribunal being RCA No. 609/2004 Learned Tribunal set aside the order being of the view that in this case tenancy rights have been conferred on the PNB by the statute because the scheme promulgated by Govt. of India vide notification dated 4.9.93 issued under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980 is legislative in character whereas the merger of the two companies in other cases namely Paras Ram Harnand Rao v. Shanti Prasad Narinder Kumar Jain; Cox and Kings Agencies Ltd and Anr. v. Smt. Chander Malhotra; General Radio and Appliances Co. Ltd. and Ors. v. M.A. Khader by LRs. was not legislative but under the provisions of companies Act. According to the learned Tribunal conferring of tenancy rights on PNB by a statute as successor to NBI does not amount to sub letting, assignment or parting with possession within the meaning of section 14(1)(b) of the Act. Accordingly, learned Tribunal set aside the eviction order. Aggrieved thereby, the landlord preferred this petition under Article 227 of the Constitution of India.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmalgamation of Oriental Bank of Commerce and United Bank of India into Punjab National Bank Scheme, 2020 (https://indiankanoon.org/doc/19585090/)\nciteturn68search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; 5)The assets of the Transferor Banks that are immovable in nature shall, be vested in or be deemed to have been vested in the Transferee Bank, without any further act or deed done or being required to be done by the Transferor Banks or by the Transferee Bank and the Transferee Bank shall be entitled to exercise all rights and privileges attached to such immovable properties and shall be liable to pay the ground rent and taxes and fulfil all obligations in relation to or applicable to such immovable properties.( ... - On and from the date of commencement of this Scheme, -(i)the Board of the Transferor Bank 1 and the Board of the Transferor Bank 2 shall stand dissolved;(ii)any whole-time director, including the managing director, of the Transferor Bank 1 and Transferor Bank 2 shall cease to hold office and shall be entitled to receive salary and allowances in lieu of the notice in accordance with the applicable law;(iii)the entire share capital of Transferor Banks shall, without any further act, deed or instrument, stand cancelled; and(iv)the shares of the Transferor Banks shall stand delisted from stock exchange in India where they are listed; and the share certificates representing such shares shall, without any further act, deed or instrument, be deemed to be automatically cancelled, extinguished and be of no effect.\n20†(2)】Upon the commencement of this Scheme, the undertakings of the Transferor Banks shall vest or be deemed to vest or be taken over by the Transferee Bank without requiring any act, deed, consent or instrument for transfer of the same.(3)On the commencement of this Scheme, in respect of such of the assets of the Transferor Banks as are movable in nature or otherwise capable of transfer by manual or constructive delivery or by endorsement and delivery, the same shall stand transferred by Transferor Banks to the Transferee Bank without requiring any deed or instrument of conveyance for transfer of the same and shall become the property of the Transferee Bank.(4)Without prejudice to the generality of sub-paragraph (1) and in respect of movable assets other than those dealt with in sub-paragraph (3), including but not limited to debts, actionable claims, earnest monies, receivables, bills, credits, loans, advances and deposits, if any, forming part of the undertaking, whether recoverable in cash or in kind or for value to be received, bank balances, etc., the same shall stand transferred to and vested in the Transferee Bank without any notice or other intimation to any person to the end and intent that the rights of the Transferor Banks to recover or realize the same stands transferred to the Transferee Bank, and to the extent such assets is a debt, loan, receivable, advance or deposit, appropriate entries should be passed in their respective books to record the aforesaid change, without any notice or other intimation to such debtors, depositors or persons as the case may be.(5)The assets of the Transferor Banks that are immovable in nature shall, be vested in or be deemed to have been vested in the Transferee Bank, without any further act or deed done or being required to be done by the Transferor Banks or by the Transferee Bank and the Transferee Bank shall be entitled to exercise all rights and privileges attached to such immovable properties and shall be liable to pay the ground rent and taxes and fulfil all obligations in relation to or applicable to such immovable properties.(6)The mutation or substitution of the title to the immovable properties shall, upon the commencement of this Scheme, be made and duly recorded in the name of the Transferee Bank.【--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIcici Bank Ltd. vs Late Smt. Shakuntla Gupta (Since ... on 12 August, 2015 (https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/157278563/?formInput=amalgamation+of+property)\nciteturn68search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Indian Kanoon - Search engine for Indian Law ... (4) If the scheme of amalgamation is approved by the requisite majority of shareholders in accordance with the provisions of this section, it shall be submitted to the Reserve Bank for sanction and shall, if sanctioned by the Reserve Bank by an order in writing passed in this behalf, be binding on the banking companies concerned and also on all the shareholders thereof. ... What, if any, is the impact of a scheme of amalgamation on the rights and obligations of the landlord and the tenant, qua the property held by the transferor-company under a contract of lease, which it may continue to occupy by virtue of the protection against eviction available to it under. 14 of the Rent Control Act, is the next question that is posed on behalf of the landlord of the commercial premises in which are house the registered office and the commercial offices of the transferor- company.\n\n> ―11. What, if any, is the impact of a scheme of amalgamation on the rights and obligations of the landlord and the tenant, qua the property held by the transferor-company under a contract of lease, which it may continue to occupy by virtue of the protection against eviction available to it under. 14 of the Rent Control Act, is the next question that is posed on behalf of the landlord of the commercial premises in which are house the registered office and the commercial offices of the transferor- company. This question raises a number of subsidiary legal problems. Are the tenancy rights of the transferor-company \"property\" within the meaning of Section 394(4)(a) of the Act and, Therefore, capable of being transferred to the transferee- company on amalgamation ? Does such a transfer tantamount in law to an assignment of the tenancy by or on behalf of the company, or amount to parting with possession, etc., and would, Therefore, be within the mischief of Section 14(1)(b) of the Rent Control Act? --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraziano Trasmissioni India Pvt ... vs Acit,(Osd), Delhi on 10 April, 2026 (https://indiankanoon.org/doc/47377564/)\nciteturn68search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: today; United Bank of India 1988 (1) SCR 1088 it was held that in every case of transfer, devolution, merger or scheme of amalgamation, in which rights and liabilities of one company are transferred or devolved upon another company, the successor-in-interest becomes entitled to the liabilities and assets of the Page | 22 transferor company subject to the terms and conditions of contract of transfer or merger, as it were. ... In response to the above notice, the Appellant informed the learned TPO in Delhi jurisdiction, of the amalgamation through its submission dated 5 September 2023 (Refer page 336 of the factual paper-book), filed in the name of GTIPL (successor of FAL) enclosing scheme of merger, order of NCLT and intimation letter to registrar of companies along with all the annexures as mentioned in the para 10 above.\n> (b) by deeming fiction-through several provisions of the Act. the treatment of various issues, is such that the transferee is deemed to carry on the enterprise as that of the transferor.\n\n29. In Bhagwan Dass Chopra v. United Bank of India 1988 (1) SCR 1088 it was held that in every case of transfer, devolution, merger or scheme of amalgamation, in which rights and liabilities of one company are transferred or devolved upon another company, the successor-in-interest becomes entitled to the liabilities and assets of the Page | 22 transferor company subject to the terms and conditions of contract of transfer or merger, as it were. Later, in Singer India Ltd v. Chander Mohan Chadha [2004] Supp (3) SCR 535 this court held as follows:\n\n> \"8...there can be no doubt that when two companies amalgamate and merge into one, the transferor company loses its identity as it ceases to have its business.\n\n> However, their respective rights and liabilities are determined under the scheme of amalgamation, but the corporate identity of transferor company ceases to exist with effect from the date the amalgamation is made effective.\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHazari Courts vs M/S Hitkari Brothers Of Delhi (Cycles) on 29 August, 2013 (https://indiankanoon.org/doc/172908545/)\nciteturn68search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago;  The plea taken up by the said Indian company that it was on the directions of the RBI issued to the American company and subsequent thereto on the orders of Bombay High Court that the American company was amalgamated with the Indian company was rejected by the Hon'ble Supreme Court and as already noted herein above it was held that whether it is a voluntary act of the tenant or otherwise and also the reasons for doing so are wholly irrelevant and can have no bearing. ... Reference herein can be made to the judgment of Hon'ble High Court of Delhi in Mrs. ... Prem Lata Bhatia was allotted in perpetuity shop no. 10 at K.G.Marg by Union of India at a monthly rent of Rs. 901/- and one of the clauses of the license deed was that the petitioner/tenant shall not permit the said premises or any part thereof being used by any other person for any purpose whatsoever without the previous consent in writing.\n The plea taken up by the said Indian company that it was on the directions of the RBI issued to the American company and subsequent thereto on the orders of Bombay High Court that the American company was amalgamated with the Indian company was rejected by the Hon'ble Supreme Court and as already noted herein above it was held that whether it is a voluntary act of the tenant or otherwise and also the reasons for doing so are wholly irrelevant and can have no bearing. Accordingly, the eviction order was passed against the respondent/tenant on the grounds of sub-letting. In the present case, as already noted, petitioner has not been able to prove that the constituents of the respondent firm are different from that of the alleged sub-tenant M/s Anil & Co. Pvt. Limited. Instead, respondent has proved on record that the two partners of the respondent firm are also the only two Directors of the alleged sub-tenant M/s Anil & Co. Pvt. Limited. Reference herein can be made to the judgment of Hon'ble High Court of Delhi in Mrs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\noriental bank of commerce (https://indiankanoon.org/search/?formInput=ori